  "question": {
    "text": "Correct order of stability of carbanion is\n<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/bitsat/RwbXgq0e98g2MbfoSQDe_I132uDFiCAYMOKyyDOpRvQ.original.fullsize.png\"/><br/>",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "fqtLV",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{C}\\gt\\mathrm{B}\\gt\\mathrm{D}\\gt\\mathrm{A}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "EQmNM",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{A}\\gt\\mathrm{B}\\gt\\mathrm{C}\\gt\\mathrm{D}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "pmuYA",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{D}\\gt\\mathrm{A}\\gt\\mathrm{C}\\gt\\mathrm{B}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "S8yHn",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{D}\\gt\\mathrm{C}\\gt\\mathrm{B}\\gt\\mathrm{A}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "As we know compound (4) is aromatic and the compound (1) is anti-aromatic. Hence compound (4) is most stable and compound (1) is least stable among these in compound (2) and (3) carbon atom having charge is $\\mathrm{sp}^3$ hybridised. On the basis of angle strain theory compound (3) is more stable than compound (2).\n<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/bitsat/afbYcdjILpHCUdx9umgUDi13TMgHFMFifpTdY5I3AkU.original.fullsize.png\"/><br/>",
    "image": null
